<h1>Privacy</h1>
<p class="lede-inline">
  Plain-language draft for the Flim apps and flim.me. Not counsel-approved.
  Controller: Panu Hällfors —
  <a href="mailto:{{ site.contactEmail }}">{{ site.contactEmail }}</a>.
</p>

<h2>The short version</h2>
<p>
  Analytics and crash reports are off until you opt in. That one choice covers
  product analytics (PostHog in the EU) and crash reports (Sentry in the EU).
  You can change it later in Settings → Analytics. Chat contents stay with you
  and your Matrix homeserver. Push notifications are separate: if you allow
  them, Flim’s gateway only gets what’s needed to wake the phone — not message
  text.
</p>

<h2>Who’s responsible</h2>
<p>
  For flim.me and for the Flim apps’ own telemetry (when you opt in), the
  controller is Panu Hällfors. See <a href="/about">about</a>.
</p>
<p>
  Separate from that:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>Your <strong>Matrix homeserver</strong> processes account and message data under its own rules.</li>
  <li>Flim’s <strong>push gateway</strong> (when used) sees the device push token plus room and event IDs, so it can wake the phone for a notification — not message bodies.</li>
  <li>If you choose to use <strong>Flim Summaries</strong> (optional), it has its own
    <a href="/summaries/privacy">privacy policy</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What the app may send (only if you opt in)</h2>
<p>
  After you agree:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Analytics</strong> — rough product usage (screens and features), to Flim’s PostHog Cloud EU project.</li>
  <li><strong>Crash reports</strong> — to Flim’s Sentry project with EU data storage, so bugs can get fixed.</li>
</ul>
<p>
  Message bodies are not treated as analytics. You can say no and keep using the
  app. Calls in the app do not send analytics.
</p>

<h2>Push notifications</h2>
<p>
  To deliver notifications, the app registers a push token with Flim’s push
  gateway. The gateway uses that token plus room and event IDs. It does not get
  message text or voice message data under the usual event-id-only setup.
</p>

<h2>This website</h2>
<p>
  flim.me does <strong>not</strong> use cookies and does <strong>not</strong>
  run site analytics. The web server may keep ordinary server logs (including
  IP addresses) for operation and abuse resistance.
</p>

<h2>Your rights</h2>
<p>
  This policy does not cover an account, a name, an email address, or your
  chats. Those are not stored here. Flim Summaries, if you use it, is
  <a href="/summaries/privacy">separate</a>.
</p>
<p>
  What can exist under this policy is limited: an IP address in ordinary host
  logs, a push token if notifications are on, and device or usage data only if
  you opt in to analytics or crash reports. For that data you can ask for
  access, correction, or deletion, object to or restrict processing, and
  complain to a supervisory authority. Write to
  <a href="mailto:{{ site.contactEmail }}">{{ site.contactEmail }}</a>.
</p>

<h2 id="analytics">Analytics and crash reports</h2>
<p>
  This section is the stable target for the app’s “learn more about analytics”
  link (<code>/privacy#analytics</code>).
</p>
<p>
  When you opt in, Flim apps send product analytics to Flim’s own PostHog
  project in the EU and crash reports to Flim’s own Sentry project in the EU.
  One consent covers both. You can withdraw in Settings →
  Analytics at any time. The website itself does not set cookies or run
  analytics for that purpose.
</p>
